A||N||15||mindscape||1988 2.5 No Catalytic Converter||||1||
Z||000000||mindscape||08-15-2001||02:48 AM||||Recently I bought a second Fiero, a 1988 2.5 Coupe with 67K miles.  However, the previous owner removed the catalytic converter, Tennessee car, and put a dual exhaust on the car - has one of those welded together mufflers, if you want to call it a muffler.  I checked the computer and factory PROM.  What does it do... it runs great out on the road, but when the car is first started... when letting the clutch out it tends to want to die if not reved a little.  By the time I roll down my long driveway it is fine for traffic.<P>So far I've replaced the EGR valve because the one on the car was BAD, vacuum leak, and had a block off plate installed.<P>Basically I want this as a dependable everyday driver; the 1988 GT project is on hold at the moment - not too happy about the noise in the new motor nor the metal in the oil  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>So, on the 1988 2.5 what would you do?  I have access to a dual exhaust off a 1987 Fiero as well as an exhuast on a 2.5 Fiero of unknown year - it's been parted out mostly.  I'm thinking about using the pipe and converter off the 2.5 car and the muffer and tail pipes off the 1987 car.  Will the 1987 muffler and tail pipes fit?  I know the tail pipes are bent different on the 1988 cars with dual exhaust.<P>Any suggestions?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Nashco||08-15-2001||03:12 AM||||First of all, a change in exhaust won't affect your car on startup enough that you would notice.  I wouldn't worry about the exhaust until first fixing this running problem.<P>Have you already checked the most basic things such as the plugs, wires, crank/cam sensor condition, and visible injector spray pattern.  Do you know what to look for?<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000002||mindscape||08-15-2001||04:39 AM||||Yes, basic issues have been considered.  However, this is my first 2.5; once swore I'd never own a four cylinder engine car - go figure.<P>What has been checked?  It has a new set of wires from the dealer - previous owner.  It has a new set of plugs.  I put a new set of Bosch in over the weekend.  K&N Air Cleaner - injector does not drip - spray pattern looks normal to me, but I'm not an expert.  Gas mileage on my first tank with this car was around 28MPG.<P>NOTE, it starts fine and idles high for a bit, then backs down to a normal idle.  After it runs for a couple of minutes it is not an issue.  -&gt; After it is warm it no longer idles high when started, but has the same behavior - wanting to stall.  Again, if I let it run for a couple of minutes -&gt; No problem.<P>With the type of exhaust that is on the car I do understand there is simply not enough back pressure for the oxygen sensor to work properly.<P>Suppose I'll pickup a spare coil-pack while I'm at the junkyard tomorrow; suppose it would be good to have on a trip.<P>I'm only human, I can miss things; so by all means tell me what to look for.  I won't take it personally, though I'm sure I have the bases covered.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by mindscape (edited 08-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Nashco||08-15-2001||05:01 AM||||OK, next things to check are all vacuum lines, which would create a surging idle.<P>The oxygen sensor does not rely on pressure whatsoever; it actually just uses a chemical reaction with oxygen to generate an electric current, which is measured by the computer and used for your fuel/spark settings.  Trust me, it's not your exhaust.<P>Chances are if it was a coil pack, cam/crank sensor, module, etc...it would do it all the time, or intermittently, rather than just at cold start.<P>Injector spray is pretty easy...basically if it looks even all the way around and doesn't drip, it's fine.  Fuel pressure may be an issue at fresh start...are you turning the key on and hearing the fuel pump cycle for 3 seconds or so?  A lot of GM vehicles run the fuel pump relay through the oil pressure switch as a secondary relay.  If relay number one goes bad, it relies solely on the oil pressure sensor for power.  I'm not sure if this is the case on your car, just a thought.  Listen to see if the fuel pump is cycling when you turn the key on (key on, engine not running).  Keep chugging away, you'll figure it out soon enough.<P>Oh, and lastly....there's not a damn thing wrong with a 4 cylinder...actually, a lot of four cylinders are becoming a little enviable compared to our old-school v6.  It's a car...who cares how many big holes the engine has?<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT<P>||||reg||4
Z||000004||mindscape||08-15-2001||06:09 AM||||Back pressure?  I'd like to know more, but remember I'm skeptical.  As I understand it there is suppose to be a certain amount of backpressure in order for an engine to run correctly, unless otherwise designed.  Further, the backpressue will change the concentration of exhaust gases that react with the oxygen sensor.  It has been my experience with other cars that removing or gutting the catalytic converter actually hurts the gas mileage because the signal the computer gets from the O2 sensor indicates a lean condition - so the software compensates by riching up the fuel air mixture to compensate.  Yet, this may improve performace, but that is not what I have in mind for this car; 1988 GT project yes...<P>I'll check for vacuum leaks when I get up this afternoon.  I did notice that the ignition coils are not a matched set.  Looks as if one has been replaced.  Suppose I could put a new set of coils on it and see what happens.  Then again, I'm broke for the next three weeks.  I'll have to see what's up at the junkyard.<P>BTW, my other car is a 1974 AMC Hornet sedan with a 360, four barrel 4350 Motorcraft spread bore carb and true dual exhaust; 300 Horse and 362.5 ft/lbs of torque.  Not bad, considering it is all STOCK; basically - no mechanial fuel pump or cooling fan, nor power stiring and brakes.  There is nothing like raw power  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  Especially with nothing but drum brakes!<p>[This message has been edited by mindscape (edited 08-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||mbramble||08-15-2001||07:04 AM||||Just FYI, I have an 87 2.5 that I removed the cat from when it got clogged.  It runs absolutely no differently now than when the cat was in good working order.  It MAY have a little less oomph off the line but if it does it is barely noticeble by me since I rarely stomp on it anyway.  I get as high as 39 MPG on the hiway depending on how I drive on a trip.||||reg||1
Z||000006||mindscape||08-15-2001||02:55 PM||||Say, if there is a noticeable miss at idle or low RPM then ... what would you check?  Coil pack?    I've been over the vacuum lines and found no leaks.<P>What you are saying about no catalytic converter is almost my experience with this car so far.  Though, the gas mileage is low, I believe.  What are the 2.5 cars rated at?||||reg||1
Z||000007||Nashco||08-15-2001||03:07 PM||||Ok...I'll go to even further pains to tell you it's *not* the exhaust.  The reason your car's performance is changed after removing the catalyst is because you change the torque properties of your engine, not because of the pressure surrounding the oxygen sensor.  The torque properties are changed because with a free flowing exhaust, the air is allowed to move quicker through the engine, allowing higher horsepower.  Torque is created by having a slower flow of air through the engine (large intake valves, small exhaust valves, for example) that ensure a long, slow, burn in comparison to the free flow setup.  This is the reason that removing the catalytic converter gives you more power at very high rpm's, but loses lots of torque in the lower rpm's.<P>Yes, your car always needs *some* backpressure to run, but the restrictions in the head and manifolds alone are enough for the car to run on its own.<P>Yet again..stop being a skeptic...it's not your exhaust, trust me <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>If I were a betting man, I'd bet against the coils; like I said, *very* rarely is a coil, module, sensor, etc...bad unless it is intermittent or heat related, which isn't what your problem is.<P>Check the vacuum lines and listen for loud leaks.  You can get a can of carb/choke cleaner and spray it around suspected leaks...listen for the engine idle rpm to pick up momentarily when spraying.  If it does, you've found a vacuum leak.<P>Oh, I was going to ask...did your car have a block off plate installed, then you put the EGR valve back on, or did you put a block off plate on it?<P>Lastly...I love the AMCs, especially the early 70s AMX!  My dad has a 72 Charger...what a beautiful car.  You know, you can get disc brakes man...do it, those cars were horrible at braking for their power and weight, yikes!<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||3
Z||000008||theogre||08-15-2001||08:02 PM||||go over all the wiring... notes in my cave...<P>pull out the MAT sensor in the manifold to the right of the TBI. If that's crapped up the thing will do odd stuff. This one often doesn't set errors when it's shot and the errors it does set are often batty.<P>check the base of the TBI for vac leaks. spray it with some wd40 or carb cleaner. if the RPM changes, it's leaking. This is really common if the car is an automatic. (read the torque strut article in my cave.)<P>Check the EGR and make sure it's tight. Check PCV line, clamp all rubber/metal joints.<P>Even when they are perfect... the DIS motor can be a little crabby when first started in the morning. <p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 08-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||mindscape||08-15-2001||11:58 PM||||Last night I noticed that one of the ignition coils was replaced.  So while I was at the junkyard today I got a set of coils.  Makes a noticable difference.<P>EGR - I replaced it because of a vacuum leak - it had a bad diaphram.  When it was taken off I found the block off plate.<P>Okay, so it is NOT the exhaust - Nashco.  But, tell me, TECHNICALLY speaking, altering the exhaust flow does affect the operation of the oxygen sensor.  Yes?  No?<P>Are there any good books on engines?  Recommendations?<p>[This message has been edited by mindscape (edited 08-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||falconhulk||08-16-2001||02:08 PM||||No the exhaust gas speed has nothing to do with the amount of gas's flowing past the sensor.  The sensor reacts with the o2 concentration that flows past it.  You will not lose "lots" of torque!!!  Why do you think that drag cars run open exaust with 0 backpressure?  HP is not the only important thing.  It takes monster torque to get off the line in a hurry.  You might lose 1 or 2 lb of torque but thats it!  You will not even notice it.  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Nashco||08-16-2001||06:06 PM||||Actually, several people on the forum have removed the catalytic converter and had a noticeable loss in torque.  1FST2M6 has actually done many different exhaust configurations and dynoed them, if you want to do a search for those.  Drag engines run at 6k plus rpms, and they don't have stock engines by any means, that's how they still have torque.  Rotating mass has more to do with your torque than exhaust backpressure; since they're running massive engines at very high rpms, torque is a natural by-product.  Not trying to discount your thinking falconhulk, just offering a different point of view.<P>The O2 sensor measure the concentration of oxygen present at it...TECHNICALLY speaking, a change in exhaust pressure would change the concentration of oxygen present, but it would be A VERY small amount in comparison to the levels already there...such a small amount it is doubtful if a standard O2 sensor would have any voltage change.<P>Oh, and good books on engines?  Go to Barnes and Noble, Books a Million, etc. and see what they have.  I normally spend hours at a time just reading the books in the store.  If a book has a LOT of great information, I buy it.  You can also look around on Amazon.com, but then you rely on the recommendations of others and what little you already know about the book.  Most factory service manuals are pretty good for diagnosis, especially the specialized ones (emissions, electronics, etc).  Reading is a great step in learning more...good for you for asking <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000012||JohnF||08-16-2001||06:43 PM||||"What are the 2.5 cars rated at?"<P>I have owned an '88 2.5 since the day it was new.  I'm over 50 and still like my cars to GO!<P>FYI - I just did a little check:  <BR>  Mostly in town - 18.75 mpg<BR>  Mostly on the highway - 24.85 mpg<P>I love this little car and got a Volvo owners good checkout today.||||reg||1
Z||000013||falconhulk||08-17-2001||08:46 AM||||I have a car that I drag.  355 HP and 345 lb/torque.  All the power is between 2500 and 4000 rpm.  No cats.  Dual exaust.  Forget what you read this is the real world.  Muscle cars had no cats.  People did not say thank god for emmison laws becasue without them we would have not torque!  They said damn my car is way slower.  No motor sport runs a cat.  There is a reason for that.  Do you know how many people think that the heat range on spark plugs effect how hot your engine runs?  A lot of myths out there..........<P>[This message has been edited by falconhulk (edited 08-17-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by falconhulk (edited 08-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||mindscape||08-17-2001||11:33 AM||||Nashco, I appreciate your argument about the exhaust and how it affects engine operation.  However, the scientist is me says to gather some data.  Using a data logger, built using a NetMedia BASICX24, I'd like to compare the operation of the oxygen sensor on a stock exhaust versus the free flow system on my car currently.  Then there would be quantative evidence as to how much a free flow effects oxygen sensor operation.  Then one could predict how this effects the air/fuel mixture setting of the ECM firmware.<P>Anybody know where I can get a disassembly of the ECM firmware L4 and V6?<P>Falconhulk, what is your point?  All I'm getting at is that the system software is setup for operation in a given range of engine conditions.  Go outside of this range and things go awry.  So, based on your post, what people think, misinformation, I'd like to separate misinformation from FACT.<P>All I need now is someone to volunteer to compare Fiero operation.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by mindscape (edited 08-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||falconhulk||08-17-2001||12:36 PM||||heh heh my point was in response to the comment mad to me about back pressure.  I was pointing out that back pressure is not the big tQ producing factor everyone thinks it is.  Sure you lose a little but not so much as everyone thinks.  Sorry if I confused you.  Read up a little and you will see what I am talking about.||||reg||1
